---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitsLifeSizeConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitsLifeSizeConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="shoe-size-converter">
  <UnitsLifeSizeConverter client:load mode="shoe" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Measure the foot, not the label</h2>
    <p>
      Shoe sizing has no international standard, and the three main systems were not designed to convert
      into one another. A label-to-label conversion adds two approximations together. A conversion from a
      measured foot length adds only one, so each row in the shoe size converter charts starts from
      centimetres.
    </p>
    <h3>How to measure</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Measure at the end of the day. Feet swell by up to half a size between morning and evening.</li>
      <li>Stand on a sheet of paper with your heel against a wall, in the socks you will wear with the shoes.</li>
      <li>Mark the paper at the tip of your longest toe, which is not always the big toe.</li>
      <li>Measure from the wall edge of the paper to the mark, in centimetres.</li>
      <li>Measure both feet and use the longer measurement. Most people have a measurable difference.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Where the shoe size systems come from</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">System</th><th scope="col">Unit</th><th scope="col">Measures</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>UK</td><td>Barleycorn, 1/3 inch</td><td>The last, starting from a zero point of 4 inches</td></tr>
        <tr><td>US</td><td>Barleycorn, 1/3 inch</td><td>The same, with a different zero, which gives the constant offset</td></tr>
        <tr><td>EU (Paris point)</td><td>2/3 cm</td><td>The last, not the foot</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Japan / Mondopoint</td><td>Millimetre or centimetre</td><td>The foot itself</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Two of the four systems measure the last, the form the shoe is built on. The last includes toe room,
      which varies by manufacturer. Only Mondopoint and Japanese sizing measure the foot, so only those two
      are predictable.
    </p>

    <h2>Common offsets</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Men: US &asymp; UK + 0.5 to 1</li>
      <li>Women: US &asymp; UK + 2</li>
      <li>Women&apos;s US &asymp; men&apos;s US + 1.5, for the same foot</li>
      <li>EU &asymp; 1.5 &times; (foot length in cm + 1.5)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      These are rules of thumb, and they drift at the ends of the range. Where they disagree with the chart,
      use the chart.
    </p>

    <h2>Width is the other half of a fit</h2>
    <p>
      Length gets the attention, but width causes most of the pain. American brands publish width letters:
      B is standard for women and D for men, and the scale runs from 2A (narrow) to 6E (extra extra wide).
      European brands seldom publish a width, though some use a G or H fitting for wide. If a shoe is the
      right length but pinches across the ball of the foot, you need a wider fitting, not a longer shoe.
    </p>

    <h2>Toe room</h2>
    <p>
      Leave 10&ndash;15 mm, about a thumb&apos;s width, between your longest toe and the end of the shoe.
      With less, downhill walking or running bruises the toenail. For this reason the chart lists a
      recommended shoe interior length next to your foot length. A shoe whose interior equals your foot
      length is too small.
    </p>

    <h2>Why brands still differ</h2>
    <p>
      Even with an exact conversion, a running brand and a dress-shoe brand can be a full size apart at the
      same label, and one brand&apos;s models vary between lasts. If a brand publishes a foot-length chart,
      use it: it describes their lasts, and this one describes the industry average. If not, buy from a
      retailer with free returns and order both half sizes.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
